# SYNTAX TEST "text.html.tiddlywiki5" "A single-line define ends at its line, parentheses or not"

# TiddlyWiki reads a define with no closing pragma as a one-line definition; the body runs to the
# end of the line and the tiddler continues beneath it.
#
# A body ENDING IN A CLOSING PARENTHESIS leaves the cursor after a parenthesis at end of line —
# exactly what the multiline branch waits for, its begin reading (?<=\))\s*$. A rule holding both
# forms at once therefore opens a block on a one-line define and swallows the rest of the file
# hunting an \end that never comes. TiddlyWiki's own documentation carries the shape:
# filters/examples/search-replace Operator (Examples) defines a regular-expression macro whose
# body reads as a group.
#
# So the LINE names which form it holds, before either branch opens. Its lookahead reads past a
# parameter list that ends at the FIRST closing parenthesis, balanced or not, because macrodef.js
# ends it there — its own regexp reads \(\s*([^)]*)\).

! A body of plain text ends at the line

\define plain() e|o

Prose after a plain body.
#<- text.html.tiddlywiki5 meta.paragraph.tiddlywiki5
#<- - meta.variable.macro.body.tiddlywiki5

! A body ending in a closing parenthesis ends there too

\define grouped() (abc)(def)

Prose after a grouped body.
#<- text.html.tiddlywiki5 meta.paragraph.tiddlywiki5
#<- - meta.variable.macro.body.tiddlywiki5

! And a define with no body on its line still opens a block

\define blockform()
the body
\end

Prose after the block.
#<- text.html.tiddlywiki5 meta.paragraph.tiddlywiki5
#<- - meta.variable.macro.body.tiddlywiki5

